architecture.md
local-development.md
dev-server.md
docker.md
database.md
backup-restore.md
ci-cd.md
security.md
troubleshooting.md
upgrade.md
```

Adapt names to existing documentation conventions.

---

# 102. ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM

Document:

```text
GitHub
   |
GitHub Actions
   |
Container Registry
   |
DEV Server
   |
Reverse Proxy
   |
+-----------------------+
|                       |
Frontend             Backend
                        |
                  +-----+------+
                  |            |
              PostgreSQL     Redis
                               |
                             Celery
```

Also document future:

```text
DEV
STAGING
PRODUCTION
```

architecture.

---

# 103. DEV VS PRODUCTION

Clearly separate:

### DEV

```text
dev.axontimehr.com
DEV database
DEV secrets
DEV email
DEV storage
DEV AI configuration
```

### PRODUCTION

```text
app.axontimehr.com
PRODUCTION database
PRODUCTION secrets
PRODUCTION email
PRODUCTION storage
PRODUCTION AI configuration
```

Never mix them.

---

# 104. OPEN-SOURCE INSTALLATION

A fresh developer should be able to follow:

```text
README
    |
    v
Prerequisites
    |
    v
Clone repository
    |
    v
Configure .env
    |
    v
docker compose up
    |
    v
Run migrations
    |
    v
Seed demo data
    |
    v
Open application
```

Document Windows, Linux, and macOS differences where necessary.

---

# 105. WINDOWS DEVELOPMENT

Since many developers may use Windows, ensure Docker-based development minimizes OS-specific setup.

Prefer:

```text
Docker Desktop
```

for Windows developers.

Avoid requiring PostgreSQL to be installed directly on Windows if Docker already provides it.

---

# 106. LINUX DEV SERVER

Document Ubuntu deployment.

The server should not require:

```text
Node.js
Python
PostgreSQL
Redis
```

to be manually installed on the host if they run inside containers.

The host should primarily need:

```text
Docker
Docker Compose
Git
```

plus system/network dependencies.

---

# 107. SERVER DIRECTORY STRUCTURE

Use a predictable deployment directory.

Example:

```text
/opt/axontimehr/
```

or another appropriate location.

Possible:

```text
/opt/axontimehr/
├── compose/
├── config/
├── backups/
├── logs/
└── scripts/
```

Do not store application secrets in Git.

---

# 108. DATA DIRECTORY SAFETY

Document:

```text
database data
uploaded files
backups
logs
```

and which locations are persistent.

Never accidentally mount:

```text
host /
```

into a container.

---

# 109. PRODUCTION-READY DATABASE SETTINGS

Do not blindly use development PostgreSQL settings in production.

Prepare configuration for:

```text
max_connections
shared_buffers
logging
autovacuum
timezone
backup
```

Do not over-optimize a small DEV server.

---

# 110. DATABASE INDEX REVIEW

Before deployment, inspect existing schema for indexes on:

```text
tenant_id
employee_id
user_id
organization_id
created_at
updated_at
attendance_date
leave dates
payroll period
foreign keys
```

Do not add indexes blindly.

Use query patterns and existing migrations.

---

# 111. STARTUP ORDER

Use health checks and dependency conditions where supported.

Expected:

```text
PostgreSQL
      |
      v
Migration
      |
      v
FastAPI
      |
      +---- Redis
      |
      v
Celery
```

Frontend can start independently once backend routing is available.

---

# 112. RESTART POLICY

Critical services should restart automatically after unexpected failure.

Example:

```text
restart: unless-stopped
```

or an equivalent appropriate strategy.

Do not hide persistent crashes through endless restart loops.

Health monitoring must detect repeated failures.

---

# 113. RESOURCE MONITORING

Create a simple operational checklist:

```text
CPU
RAM
Disk
PostgreSQL size
Redis memory
Docker container status
Celery queue
Backup status
SSL status
```

Document how developers can inspect them.

---

# 114. APPLICATION SHUTDOWN

Ensure FastAPI and Celery shut down gracefully.

Do not abruptly terminate database operations unnecessarily.

---

# 115. DEPENDENCY PINNING

Review:

```text
requirements.txt
pyproject.toml
package.json
package-lock.json
```

Use appropriate lock/pinning mechanisms.

Do not upgrade every dependency simply because Phase 14 is being implemented.

---

# 116. DATABASE MIGRATION TEST

Create a CI test:

```text
empty PostgreSQL
       |
       v
all migrations
       |
       v
latest schema
       |
       v
application startup
```

This verifies that a new installation works.

---

# 117. EXISTING DATABASE MIGRATION TEST

Also test:

```text
previous schema
       |
       v
new migration
       |
       v
latest schema
```

This protects existing deployments.

---

# 118. API SMOKE TESTS

After deployment verify critical APIs:

```text
health
login
current-user
tenant
employee
attendance
leave
payroll
notification
```

Use only safe demo accounts.

---

# 119. UI SMOKE TEST

Verify:

```text
Application loads
Login works
Dashboard loads
Navigation works
API calls succeed
Logout works
```

If Playwright is already configured, reuse it.

Do not create a completely separate UI automation framework.

---

# 120. SECURITY SMOKE TEST

Verify:

```text
unauthenticated API blocked
invalid token rejected
expired token rejected
Tenant A cannot access Tenant B
employee cannot access admin endpoints
regular user cannot access protected configuration
```

---

# 121. PERFORMANCE BASELINE

Establish basic DEV performance metrics:

```text
frontend load
login response
dashboard response
employee list
attendance list
database query time
```

Do not perform unrealistic load testing against a small free DEV server.

---

# 122. API TIMEOUTS

Configure sensible:

```text
proxy timeout
FastAPI timeout
database timeout
Redis timeout
Celery task timeout
```

Long-running jobs should use Celery rather than keeping HTTP requests open indefinitely.

---

# 123. FILE UPLOAD LIMITS

Configure consistent limits across:

```text
Reverse Proxy
FastAPI
Application
Object storage
```

Avoid one layer allowing 500 MB while another allows 10 MB.

---

# 124. CACHING

Ensure frontend and API caching does not expose tenant-specific data.

Particularly review:

```text
dashboard
employee data
reports
documents
AI analytics
```

Do not cache private responses publicly.

---

# 125. COOKIE SECURITY

For authentication cookies where applicable:

```text
Secure
HttpOnly
SameSite
```

must be configured according to the deployment architecture.

---

# 126. CSRF

If cookie-based authentication is used, verify CSRF protection.

Do not assume JWT automatically eliminates every CSRF scenario.

---

# 127. OBJECT STORAGE SECURITY

If S3-compatible storage is implemented:

* private buckets
* signed URLs
* restricted credentials
* tenant-aware paths
* lifecycle policies
* encryption

Never expose private employee documents through public bucket URLs.

---

# 128. EMAIL SECURITY

Never include sensitive information unnecessarily in emails.

Payslips/documents should use secure access mechanisms rather than attaching sensitive documents blindly.

---

# 129. DATA PRIVACY

The DEV environment must contain:

```text
fake/demo data
```

only.

Do not copy production employee information into DEV.

If production-like data is ever required for debugging, document anonymization requirements.

---

# 130. AUDIT LOGGING

Deployment and administrative operations should be auditable where the application supports it.

Important events:

```text
configuration changes
user creation
role changes
security changes
data export
database administration
document access
```

---

# 131. AI DATA SAFETY

The DEV deployment must preserve Phase 13 AI restrictions.

AI must not:

* receive unrestricted database access
* execute arbitrary SQL
* bypass tenant isolation
* bypass RBAC
* expose employee secrets
* make autonomous employment decisions

---

# 132. NO RAW AI SQL

Maintain the Phase 13 rule:

```text
AI
 |
 v
Controlled analytics/query specification
 |
 v
Validated backend query layer
 |
 v
PostgreSQL
```

Never:

```text
AI
 |
 v
raw SQL
 |
 v
production database
```

---

# 133. BACKUP SECURITY

Database backups may contain extremely sensitive HR data.

Therefore:

* encrypt backups
* restrict access
* avoid GitHub storage
* avoid public URLs
* use retention policies
* test restoration

---

# 134. DEVELOPMENT DATABASE RESET

Provide a safe DEV reset process.

Example:

```text
reset development database
```

must clearly state:

```text
THIS DESTROYS DEV DATA
```

Never expose this capability in production.

---

# 135. FEATURE FLAGS

Prepare environment-aware feature flags.

Example:

```env
ENABLE_AI=true
ENABLE_BETA_FEATURES=true
ENABLE_DEMO_DATA=false
```

Do not use feature flags as a security boundary.

Authorization must still be enforced by backend RBAC.

---

# 136. EDITION SUPPORT

Prepare architecture for future:

```text
Community Edition
Enterprise Edition
SaaS Edition
```

Do not duplicate the entire application.

Use:

```text
configuration
feature flags
entitlements
subscription checks
```

where appropriate.

Do not accidentally expose commercial-only features in an open-source release.

---

# 137. OPEN-SOURCE COMMUNITY

Prepare:

```text
CONTRIBUTING.md
```

including:

```text
development setup
branch strategy
coding standards
tests
PR process
commit guidelines
security reporting
```

---

# 138. ISSUE TEMPLATES

Create templates for:

```text
Bug report
Feature request
Deployment issue
Security issue
```

Security vulnerabilities should NOT be encouraged to be publicly posted in normal issues.

---

# 139. PULL REQUEST TEMPLATE

Include:

```text
Summary
Implementation
Tests
Database changes
Security impact
Breaking changes
Deployment impact
Documentation
```

---

# 140. CHANGELOG

Use categories:

```text
Added
Changed
Fixed
Security
Deprecated
Removed
```

---

# 141. DEPLOYMENT STATUS

After deployment, expose or document:

```text
Application version
Commit SHA
Deployment timestamp
Environment
```

Do not expose secrets.

---

# 142. RELEASE ARTIFACTS

A release should ideally provide:

```text
source code
Docker image
migration version
CHANGELOG
deployment instructions
```

---

# 143. CI/CD PIPELINE STAGES

Implement logically separated jobs:

```text
validate
test-backend
test-frontend
security
build
publish
deploy-dev
smoke-test
```

Do not make the entire pipeline one giant shell script.

---

# 144. FAILURE HANDLING

If:

```text
tests fail
```

do not deploy.

If:

```text
Docker build fails
```

do not deploy.

If:

```text
migration fails
```

stop deployment.

If:

```text
health check fails
```

mark deployment failed.

If:

```text
smoke test fails
```

trigger rollback or clearly mark deployment unsuccessful according to the implemented strategy.

---

# 145. DATABASE MIGRATION FAILURE

Never automatically execute destructive recovery such as:

```text
DROP TABLE
DROP DATABASE
```

A migration failure must preserve the database for investigation.

---

# 146. DEPLOYMENT LOCK AND DATABASE LOCK

Prevent simultaneous migration execution.

Only one deployment process should execute migrations at a time.

---

# 147. DEV SERVER COST OPTIMIZATION

The DEV architecture should work on a small cloud VM.

Optimize for:

```text
low memory
low CPU
low storage
```

without sacrificing reliability.

Avoid unnecessary infrastructure such as Kubernetes at this stage.

Use:

```text
Docker Compose
```

for the first live DEV deployment.

---

# 148. FUTURE CLOUD MIGRATION

Do not make the application dependent on one provider.

Prepare for:

```text
Oracle Cloud
AWS
Azure
Google Cloud
DigitalOcean
Hetzner
other Linux VPS
```

The application should primarily require:

```text
Linux
Docker
Docker Compose
domain
persistent storage
```

---

# 149. STAGING PREPARATION

Create deployment configuration that can eventually support:

```text
DEV
STAGING
PRODUCTION
```

without copying the entire codebase.

Only configuration should differ where possible.

---

# 150. PRODUCTION PREPARATION

Do NOT deploy production as part of this phase unless explicitly requested.

However, ensure Phase 14 leaves the project ready for:

```text
production PostgreSQL
managed Redis
object storage
load balancer
monitoring
autoscaling
```

---

# 151. FINAL REPOSITORY REVIEW

After implementation, inspect the repository again.

Verify:

```text
No duplicate configuration
No hardcoded localhost
No secrets
No passwords
No production data
No broken imports
No broken migrations
No broken Docker builds
No broken tests
No accidental public ports
No tenant isolation regressions
```

---

# 152. REQUIRED TEST MATRIX

Execute at minimum:

## Backend

```text
pytest
lint
typecheck
```

## Frontend

```text
lint
typecheck
test
build
```

## Database

```text
fresh migration
existing migration
seed
application startup
```

## Docker

```text
build
start
health
restart
shutdown/start
```

## Security

```text
auth
RBAC
tenant isolation
IDOR
CORS
secret scanning
```

## Deployment

```text
GitHub CI
Docker build
image publish
DEV deployment
health check
smoke test
```

---

# 153. ACCEPTANCE TEST

The phase is successful only if this workflow works:

```text
Developer changes code
        |
        v
git add .
        |
        v
git commit
        |
        v
git push
        |
        v
GitHub
        |
        v
